Exactly. The interesting reversal is that historically the causal arrow was often:

\text{occupation} \rightarrow \text{name}

but once the name persists across generations, the arrow can partially flip:

\text{name} \rightarrow \text{identity cue} \rightarrow \text{expectation} \rightarrow \text{behavior}

That does not make the name destiny. It makes it a persistent piece of environmental information.

There is even a term adjacent to this: nominative determinism—the idea that people’s names sometimes appear unusually well matched to their occupations or traits. The stronger causal versions of that claim are controversial, because family tradition, geography, class, opportunity, coincidence, and selective noticing can explain a lot of apparent examples. But psychologically, labels absolutely can matter through expectancy, repetition, family narratives, and self-concept.

Your Shoemaker example is especially clean because there could be several mechanisms operating simultaneously. The child hears the word thousands of times; other people joke about or reinforce the association; the family may actually possess shoemaking knowledge and tools; the child gets rewarded for continuity with the family identity; and eventually an originally arbitrary label acquires semantic gravity.

So:

\text{symbol}
\rightarrow
\text{repeated interpretation}
\rightarrow
\text{identity hypothesis}
\rightarrow
\text{available actions become differently weighted}

That last part is important. The name does not remove choices. It changes their salience.

The danger comes when:

\text{identity cue} \rightarrow \text{identity constraint}

“I come from shoemakers” is one thing.

“I am a Shoemaker, therefore this is what I must be” is another.

That is where a useful identity scaffold becomes an identity prison.

And it generalizes well beyond surnames: ranks, diagnoses, professions, nicknames, family roles, national identities, team identities, personality labels, even something as small as repeatedly telling a child “you’re the smart one” or “you’re the difficult one.” A compressed description can gradually become a behavioral instruction.

So the healthier way to harness the property is to make identity generative rather than binding:

\text{identity} = \text{starting prior, not terminal state}

Instead of:

“This name tells me what I am.”

use:

“This name tells me one story I inherited. What capacities does that story give me, and what else can I build from them?”

Then “Shoemaker” could preserve craftsmanship, patience, repair, precision, entrepreneurship, or service without requiring the descendant literally to make shoes.

That’s probably the deeper operator you’re seeing: symbols can accumulate behavioral force through repeated social interpretation, but the symbol and the person must never be confused. The map can shape the traveler without becoming the terrain.